---
/*
  Archive view for the Portfolio grid.

  Was a button in the corner of every page. That was the wrong place for it:
  as Chris put it, the idea isn't developed enough to be site furniture, and a
  control that recolours the entire site sitting on a case study is a gimmick
  rather than a feature. So it lives on the Portfolio page now, as one more
  button in the row that already holds the filters, Shuffle and the size
  controls - a view mode alongside the other ways of looking at the grid.

  How it works, and why this way:

  Images are pushed to greyscale, then contrast is cranked, then they are
  multiplied over the paper colour. Multiply means white areas take the paper
  colour and dark areas stay dark, which is exactly what one ink on coloured
  stock does. Crucially this is all `filter` and `mix-blend-mode` - both
  compositor-friendly - rather than SVG filters, which Chrome does not
  GPU-accelerate and which would visibly stall a 66-tile grid.

  Threshold is `contrast()` taken far past 100%, with `brightness()` shifting
  where the cut falls. That is the cheap 1-bit trick and it is tunable per
  image, which matters: thresholding destroys low-contrast work, so anything
  photographic needs a softer setting or none at all.

  The choice is written to <html> before first paint by an inline script, so
  reloading in archive view doesn't flash the colour grid first.
*/

/*
  Stock colours, named after the reference zines. `ink` is deliberately
  near-black rather than pure black - slightly lifted reads more like real press
  ink and less like a screenshot.

  Only `press` is reachable today. The riso stocks below are shelved rather than
  deleted: the treatment works and is worth keeping, but picking a paper colour
  is a second idea on top of the archive view and it isn't developed enough to
  put in front of a client. Restoring the picker is a matter of rendering these
  as swatches again - nothing else has to change.
*/
interface Props {
  /*
    Where to go when the switch turns ON, and when it turns OFF.

    The archive view of the portfolio is not a restyle of the grid, it is a
    different document - /catalog, which carries citations the grid never
    fetches. So on the Portfolio page the switch navigates there rather than
    trying to rearrange tiles Isotope has absolutely positioned, and on the
    catalog it navigates back.

    Direction matters, which is why these are two props rather than one URL.
    On /portfolio only turning ON should move you; on /catalog only turning
    OFF should. A single prop would send someone who lands on the catalog
    with archive already off back to a page they did not ask for.

    Both optional: everywhere else the switch is just a switch.
  */
  navigateOn?: string;
  navigateOff?: string;
}

const {navigateOn, navigateOff} = Astro.props;

const PAPERS = [
  /*
    Press is the archive: plain white stock, black ink, no colour cast. Chris
    wanted the black-and-white archive view and the riso zine treatment to be
    one idea rather than two controls, and a white paper stock is what makes
    that work - the archive is simply print mode on white.
  */
  { id: 'press', label: 'Press', paper: '#ffffff', ink: '#0d0d0d' },
  { id: 'bone', label: 'Bone', paper: '#f4efe3', ink: '#141110' },
  { id: 'blood', label: 'Blood', paper: '#e5473c', ink: '#150605' },
  { id: 'acid', label: 'Acid', paper: '#c3e84f', ink: '#101504' },
  { id: 'shock', label: 'Shock', paper: '#f45fa4', ink: '#17040d' },
  { id: 'ice', label: 'Ice', paper: '#a9c9e8', ink: '#040b15' },
];
---

{/*
  A switch, not a button.

  It was a `pf-btn` in the same row as the filters, and identical in size,
  outline and colour - so a control that changes how the whole page renders read
  as a fifth way to narrow the list. Chris noticed it as "the archive mode
  becomes such an afterthought", which is exactly what identical form does to
  two different kinds of control.

  So it takes the shape of the thing it is. A track and a thumb is the one
  widget everybody already reads as a mode, and putting it at the end of the row
  rather than among the filters says the same thing again with position.

  role="switch" rather than a pressed button, because that is what this is - on
  or off, not selected or unselected. Screen readers announce it as a switch and
  say "on"/"off" instead of "pressed".
*/}
<button
  type="button"
  class="view-switch"
  id="ink-toggle"
  role="switch"
  aria-checked="false"
  title="Black-and-white archive view"
>
  <span class="view-switch__track" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="view-switch__thumb"></span>
  </span>
  <span class="view-switch__label">Archive</span>
</button>

{/* Inline and un-bundled so it runs before the first paint. */}
<script is:inline define:vars={{ PAPERS, navigateOn, navigateOff }}>
  (() => {
    const root = document.documentElement;
    const KEY = 'rdc-ink';

    const apply = (state) => {
      if (!state || !state.on) {
        root.removeAttribute('data-ink');
        root.removeAttribute('data-ink-grain');
        root.style.removeProperty('--ink-paper');
        root.style.removeProperty('--ink-ink');
        return;
      }
      const paper = PAPERS.find((p) => p.id === state.paper) ?? PAPERS[0];
      root.setAttribute('data-ink', paper.id);
      root.style.setProperty('--ink-paper', paper.paper);
      root.style.setProperty('--ink-ink', paper.ink);
      /*
        Grain is not a setting any more. Paper has tooth; being asked whether
        you would like some was a question with one sensible answer, and it cost
        a control in a panel that should be as small as possible.
      */
      root.setAttribute('data-ink-grain', 'on');
    };

    /*
      Swap in authored black-and-white alternates.

      Any image carrying data-archive-src has a hand-thresholded version, which
      beats anything the CSS filter can do - a computed threshold flattens
      midtones, whereas these were drawn. Images without one keep the filter, so
      this gets better as more are made and never breaks while they aren't.

      Swapped rather than rendered as a hidden second <img>, because a hidden
      image is still fetched in most browsers and that would mean every visitor
      downloading two portfolios to look at one. The original src is stashed on
      first swap so turning print mode off restores it exactly.
    */
    /*
      Anything that has to re-measure when print mode changes listens for this.

      The portfolio grid is laid out by Isotope from measured tile heights, and
      archive mode changes those heights - gutters appear, marks get inset. A
      custom event keeps that as one line in each component rather than InkMode
      knowing what a grid is.
    */
    const announce = (on) => {
      document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('inkmodechange', {detail: {on}}));
    };

    const swapArchive = (on) => {
      for (const img of document.querySelectorAll('img[data-archive-src]')) {
        if (on) {
          if (!img.dataset.originalSrc) {
            img.dataset.originalSrc = img.getAttribute('src') || '';
            img.dataset.originalSrcset = img.getAttribute('srcset') || '';
          }
          img.setAttribute('src', img.dataset.archiveSrc);
          // The archive file is one fixed rendering, so any srcset from the
          // colour version would point the browser back at colour candidates.
          img.removeAttribute('srcset');
          img.removeAttribute('sizes');
        } else if (img.dataset.originalSrc) {
          img.setAttribute('src', img.dataset.originalSrc);
          if (img.dataset.originalSrcset) img.setAttribute('srcset', img.dataset.originalSrcset);
        }
      }
    };

    /*
      `press` - white stock, black ink - because that is the archive Chris
      described: a black-and-white index of the marks. The warmer riso stocks
      are shelved, so a `paper` left in localStorage by an older visit is
      ignored rather than resurrecting a pink page nobody can now turn off.
    */
    let state = { on: false, paper: 'press' };
    try {
      const saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY) || 'null');
      if (saved) state = { ...state, on: Boolean(saved.on) };
    } catch {}
    apply(state);

    const save = () => {
      try {
        localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(state));
      } catch {}
    };

    // The button is below this script in the document, so wait for it.
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
      const toggle = document.getElementById('ink-toggle');
      if (!toggle) return;

      const sync = () => {
        // aria-checked, because this is a switch rather than a pressed button.
        toggle.setAttribute('aria-checked', String(!!state.on));
        toggle.classList.toggle('is-on', !!state.on);
      };
      sync();

      // Runs on load as well as on click, so arriving already in archive view
      // shows the marks rather than waiting for a toggle.
      swapArchive(state.on);
      announce(state.on);

      toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
        state.on = !state.on;

        /*
          Save BEFORE navigating, and navigate before touching the DOM.

          The destination reads this same localStorage key in its own
          pre-paint script, so the new page comes up already in archive view
          with no flash of the colour version. Repainting this page first
          would only be a restyle the visitor sees for one frame on their way
          out of it.
        */
        save();
        const dest = state.on ? navigateOn : navigateOff;
        if (dest) {
          window.location.href = dest;
          return;
        }

        apply(state);
        swapArchive(state.on);
        sync();
        announce(state.on);
      });
    });
  })();
</script>
